    "answer": {
      "short": "The inductive step fails from $P(1)$ to $P(2)$: the two one-horse groups have no horse in common, so their colours need not agree.",
      "worked": "The base case $P(1)$ is true. However, consider the inductive step when $k=1$. From two horses, removing each horse in turn gives two singleton groups. Each singleton has one colour, but the groups have no horse in common, so nothing establishes that those colours are equal.\n\nThe argument implicitly requires a horse shared by both groups to link their colours. This overlap is absent for two horses. Thus $P(1)$ does not establish $P(2)$, and the induction fails.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
